exports[`chapter review must cite the current body and the chapter evidence, not generic approval 1`] = `
"Return section_checks with exactly one entry for every narrative section: {\\"section_id\\":\\"exact ID\\",\\"verdict\\":\\"passed|failed|unclear\\",\\"analysis_excerpt\\":\\"exact nonempty quotation from a body paragraph in this section, or empty only when failing\\",\\"evidence_ids\\":[\\"IDs supporting the section\\"],\\"rationale\\":\\"specific editorial and evidentiary assessment\\",\\"required_changes\\":[\\"concrete revision\\"]}. Read the full report as a professional analysis, not a discussion draft. For every section assess whether named actors, dated developments and explicit operating or experimental conditions establish concrete findings; whether comparable alternatives are assessed on stated dimensions (or their non-comparability is explained); whether the reasoning from cited observations to the conclusion is explicit; and whether counterevidence, scope and decision implications are addressed where relevant. Generic advice, slogans, first-person assertions, paraphrased chapter answers and lists of source titles do not establish analytical depth. Do not demand invented numbers or mechanically require every dimension in every section. A passing section needs an exact body excerpt and relevant evidence IDs; explain what the excerpt establishes and where the cited evidence supports or limits it. Failed or unclear sections require concrete changes and must also fail or make unclear the corresponding report_checks criterion. Useful relationships must be explained in the body; redundant relationship diagrams must be removed. Check exact evidence citations beside the statements they support. Never reward a report for adding repetitive text, figures or headings."
`;

exports[`research authors receive the professional editorial standard 1`] = `
"Organize the report around the reader's decision and the evidence available, not a fixed consulting-style outline.\\nDefine the research object, timeframe, comparison set and selection criteria before ranking alternatives. For technology trends distinguish research activity, investment, talent, deployment and economic outcomes; attention is not adoption and adoption is not realized value.\\nThe executive synthesis must stand alone: state the principal answer, the strongest supporting observations, the consequence for the reader and the decisive uncertainty. Develop those observations in the body instead of repeating the abstract, takeaways and conclusion in different words.\\nUse chapter headings that state a bounded finding where evidence permits. Develop each finding through observable facts, an explicit comparison or mechanism, an inference and its implications. Identify the actors, dates, baseline and relevant conditions. A thematic label or a slogan is not a finding.\\nFor comparisons disclose units, denominator, observation period, sample, workload and measurement basis where relevant. Separate observed outcomes, self-reported results, estimates, scenarios and forecasts. Do not infer causation from group differences or compare unlike experiments as a ranking. Unknown values stay unknown; explain how missing evidence affects the comparison.\\nTranslate technical changes into the consequences relevant to the brief: performance, reliability, deployment constraints, costs, demand, supply or value distribution. Quantitative scenarios need cited inputs, explicit assumptions and a reproducible calculation; do not fabricate market sizes, ROI or precision. Where inputs are unavailable, give a conditional mechanism and the missing variables.\\nEach exhibit needs a specific analytical purpose, a bounded takeaway caption, sources and the relevant definitions or caveats beside it. Data charts require compatible measurements; comparison tables require meaningful common dimensions; write useful dependencies, conditions and failure paths as developed prose in the relevant paragraph. Do not generate relationship diagrams or boxes around paraphrased conclusions. Discuss what the exhibit establishes and what it cannot establish in adjacent prose.\\nUse relevant original figures or sourced charts alongside developed prose where they add evidence; there is no quota for illustration types. Choose exhibits from the analytical problem, not a decorative quota. Inspect available source pages and reuse relevant original diagrams with exact version and page references, an explanation and a limitation. Never substitute invented numerical charts for missing measurements.\\nDevelop every requested research question to decision-useful depth: explain the technical or economic mechanism, compare the relevant alternatives on explicit dimensions, examine the strongest concrete examples and contrary observations, and spell out implications and unresolved tests. Use several developed paragraphs where the subject requires them; a chapter answer plus a compact source summary is insufficient. Add substantive analysis rather than repetition or word-count padding. Place original figures beside the reasoning they support and make transitions explain why the next question follows.\\nCite exact saved evidence at the relevant sentence with {{evidence:EVIDENCE_ID}} markers. The reader renders these as clickable superscripts. Do not put internal IDs in prose or tables, or append repeated evidence cards to chapters.\\nConclude with implications derived from the findings. Recommendations specify who can act, what action follows, the prerequisites and the signal that would change the decision. Do not force investment advice into a technical brief or invent an action plan when evidence supports only further verification.\\nKeep acquisition logs, review status, evidence inventories and repeated accessibility descriptions outside the analytical narrative. Keep material uncertainties beside the affected conclusions, with method and references available separately. Use original prose; reference reports supply methodological examples, not facts about the current research topic or institution-branded wording."
`;
